1. SDLC PhasesRequirement Gathering & Analysis: Collects business needs and defines software scope.Design: Creates the software architecture and system specs.Development / Coding: Translates design requirements into working code.Testing: Detects bugs and verifies that the software meets quality standards.Deployment: Releases the finalized software to the production environment.Maintenance: Fixes bugs, performs updates, and maintains performance over time.2. Agile vs. WaterfallFeatureAgileWaterfallApproachIterative and flexible; divides project into small cycles.Linear and sequential; completes one phase before starting the next.RequirementsEvolving; changes can be adapted throughout the project.Fixed; requirements are set upfront and hard to change later.DeliveryFrequent, working software releases after every sprint.Single final release at the very end of the project pipeline.3. Main Scrum RolesProduct Owner: Defines product goals and prioritizes work in the product backlog.Scrum Master: Facilitates the Scrum process, removes team blockers, and enforces agile practices.Development Team: Cross-functional group that designs, builds, and tests the actual product increment.4. SprintA Sprint is a repeatable, fixed-length timebox during which a Scrum team delivers a specific set of completed work items. Its typical duration ranges from 1 to 4 weeks (most commonly 2 weeks).5. Jira Board ColumnsBacklog: A prioritized master list of all features, bugs, and tasks waiting to be worked on in future sprints.To Do: Tasks selected for the active sprint that are ready to be worked on but not yet started.In Progress: Work items currently being worked on by developers or testers.Done: Items that are completely coded, tested, verified, and meet the definition of ready for release.
